# The v12 INTEGRATION worker image: the selected provider artefact, one
# entrypoint further on.
#
# W110935, `work/records/2026/09/finding-v12-integration-worker-workload/`.
# Every line carries the decision behind it, as W6633's and W39357's recipes
# do, because each one is either inherited from a measured fact or is a
# deliberate departure from the image it is derived from.
#
# THE BASE IS AN EXPLICITLY SELECTED EXISTING PROVIDER IMAGE, AND IT HAS NO
# DEFAULT. `Dockerfile.claude` installs the provider runtime and the system
# trust store over a moving `apt-get` and a moving `npm install`, and W55361
# measured that two builds of an unchanged tree differ in exactly those two
# layers. Reinstalling them here would mint a SECOND unreviewed provider
# installation for the same deployment and make "which runtime answered" a
# question with two answers.
#
# So the operand is required rather than defaulted: a build must name the
# digest the deployment has already validated and selected, and a recipe that
# carried a floating tag would let an unselected artefact become the base by
# omission. Approver event 55641's rule is unchanged by this file -- building
# an image here produces a CANDIDATE, and selecting a digest is a separate
# deployment act that this recipe cannot perform and does not claim.
ARG PROVIDER_BASE
FROM ${PROVIDER_BASE}

# THE WORKER MODULES, RE-COPIED FROM THE REVIEWED TREE ON PURPOSE.
#
# `baton_worker.py`, `claude_agent.py` and the profile package are already in
# the selected base, and copying them again is not redundancy: it makes byte
# identity with this tree a property of THIS artefact rather than an inherited
# assumption about the base. W39357's own gate paid for that lesson -- an image
# that asserted a file merely EXISTED passed every case after the adapter it
# was certifying had changed underneath it, and byte identity is the
# discriminator between a rebuilt candidate and a stale cached one.
#
# `baton_worker.py` travels because this entry reuses its launch readers, and
# the frozen contract document travels with it: a module whose data was left
# behind is one that imports cleanly and fails at the first call that needs it.
#
# NOTHING FROM `baton_v12` IS COPIED IN, exactly as in the two images before
# this one: a worker that could import the manager is a worker one bug away
# from holding the manager's capabilities. `integration_contract.py` is
# standard-library-only for that reason and this recipe is where that pays --
# there is no manager, Authority or store package here for it to have needed.
#
# THE BUILD CONTEXT IS `v12`, NOT `v12/worker`, and every path below is spelled
# from there: the image needs material from two sibling directories and a
# context cannot reach above itself.
COPY worker/baton_worker.py /opt/baton/baton_worker.py
COPY worker/claude_agent.py /opt/baton/claude_agent.py
COPY worker/integration_contract.py /opt/baton/integration_contract.py
COPY worker/integration_workload.py /opt/baton/integration_workload.py
COPY worker/integration_entry.py /opt/baton/integration_entry.py
COPY worker/worker-control-1.0.schema.json \
     /opt/baton/worker-control-1.0.schema.json

# THE SOURCE PROFILES TRAVEL BECAUSE `claude_agent` IMPORTS THEM, and under the
# top-level name the image gives them. W71917's rule is kept intact: this
# package imports nothing from `baton_v12` and `baton_v12.worker_manager`
# imports nothing from it, which `tests/manager/test_source_boundary` holds in
# both directions. No `baton_v12` package directory is created here, so the
# image still has no way to spell the manager's namespace.
COPY python/src/baton_v12/source_profiles /opt/baton/source_profiles

# THE SAME FIXED NON-ROOT PAIR as every other image in this campaign and as the
# adapter's `--user` restriction. Restated rather than inherited: the identity
# this container runs as is a property a reviewer should be able to read in the
# recipe that produced it, and `read_launch`'s read-only proof depends on this
# process not being root.
USER 65532:65532

# ONE SHOT, EXEC FORM, NO SHELL IN THE PROCESS TREE. The manager stops this
# container by signalling PID 1, and PID 1 is Python. `integration_entry.py`
# is the composition -- a reviewable artefact rather than a shell incantation.
ENTRYPOINT ["python3", "/opt/baton/integration_entry.py"]

# Deterministic bytes and an explicit module path, as in the two images before
# this one. `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE` is for the interpreter's OWN imports
# inside the container and is NOT the cache boundary: `claude_agent` composes
# its children's environments member by member rather than inheriting this
# process's, so `PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX` there is what actually bounds a child's
# bytecode.
ENV PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 \
    PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 \
    PYTHONPATH=/opt/baton \
    PYTHONHASHSEED=0 \
    NODE_NO_WARNINGS=1

# THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `ENV ANTHROPIC_*` OF ANY KIND, and the reason is
# sharper than "secrets don't belong in images". The provider resolves its
# credential from the environment FIRST: an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` present here
# would silently outrank the mounted slot and decide which account every
# container built from this image ran as. The adapter composes the child's
# environment itself and forwards nothing, and this image inherits that posture
# rather than adding a fallback to it.
#
# There is also no `ENV BATON_WORKER_*`: that transport was retired with no
# fallback (W26291), and this image's programs do not read those names at all.
#
# No `VOLUME`, no `EXPOSE`, no `HEALTHCHECK`: the assignment, the result, the
# bundle and the target are mounts the manager names per attempt, and this
# container's health is observed by the adapter through the engine rather than
# announced by the container.
